  "analysis": "We are given multiple datasets describing convex polygons (islands). For each island, the distance to the sea from a point inside equals its distance to the polygon boundary (the nearest edge), because the sea lies outside the convex island. The problem reduces to finding the largest possible distance from a point inside the polygon to its boundary, i.e., the inradius of the convex polygon (the radius of the largest inscribed circle) and reporting that distance.\n\nA robust approach is to binary-search the inradius d and, for each candidate d, check if there exists a point inside the island that is at least distance d away from every edge. Each edge defines a half-plane of points whose distance to that edge is at least d. Intersecting all these half-planes yields the feasible region for the center of the inscribed circle. If the intersection is non-empty, d is feasible.\n\nTo implement this efficiently and robustly, use a simple half-plane clipping (Sutherland–Hodgman) approach:\n- Start with a large bounding polygon (a big axis-aligned square).\n- For each edge i, compute the inward normal and the offset c' = n·p_i + d|e|, where e is the edge vector and n is the inward normal (-dy, dx).\n- Clip the current polygon with the half-plane a*x + b*y >= c' where a=n.x, b=n.y and c'=n·p_i + d|e|.\n- If after clipping all edges the polygon remains non-empty, d is feasible.\n\nWe also precompute an upper bound for d by considering, for each edge, the maximum projection along the inward normal across all vertices; the inradius cannot exceed the minimum of these per-edge maxima.\n\nAlgorithm outline per dataset:\n- Read n and the n vertices in CCW order.\n- For each edge i: compute dx, dy, edge length L, inward normal n = (-dy, dx), and signature s_i = n·p_i. Compute per-edge max projection across all vertices max_s; set upper bound contribution M_i = (max_s - s_i) / L. d_upper = min_i M_i (clamped to >=0).\n- Binary search d in [0, d_upper], using about 60 iterations for precision. For each d, perform polygon clipping as described; feasibility if non-empty.\n- Output the obtained d with 6 decimals (sufficient to meet 1e-5 error bound).\n\nComplexity: Each feasibility check clips a polygon (starting with 4 points) by up to n half-planes; overall time is O(n * m) where m is the number of vertices in the clipped polygon (bounded and typically small for convex inputs). With 60 iterations, this easily fits within limits for n ≤ 100.\n",
